#{[17592186299640 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}=2\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =T\\left(2\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =2T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -22\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302202 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.5){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.5,{u}_{33}=1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=0.5.$"] [17592186302267 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3-(-3) & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3-(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-6{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}\\right),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302268 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}\\right),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302309 "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)U \\\\ C & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302328 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.25){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}B & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ B & =\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)U \\\\ B & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.25,{u}_{33}=-3,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-0.75.$"] [17592186302329 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $K$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $K$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{7}{4}\\right){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}K=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}K & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}K & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ K & =\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)U \\\\ K & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302331 "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & ={s}_{11}{C}_{11}+{s}_{12}{C}_{12}+{s}_{13}{C}_{13}+{s}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+2\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =2{C}_{12} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & 2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 1 & ∗ & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & ∗ & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\left(1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =-2((-5\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 2)-0+0) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302372 "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & ={t}_{11}{C}_{11}+{t}_{12}{C}_{12}+{t}_{13}{C}_{13}+{t}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+4\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =-{C}_{11}+4{C}_{13} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 4 & ∗ \\\\ 9 & 0 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & ∗ & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & ∗ & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+4\\left(9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =-0+4\\cdot 9(6\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =36\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-432\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308357 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\right)+\\begin{bmatrix}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ -2{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{2} \\\\ -2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}({a}_{2}-2)t+({b}_{2}+1) \\\\ (-2{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}+8)t+(-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}-2 &=0 \\\\ -2{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}+8 &=0 \\\\ {b}_{2}+1 &={a}_{1} \\\\ -2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} &={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first two equations, we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2,\\,{a}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=0,\\,{b}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308358 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\right)+\\begin{bmatrix}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-3)t+(2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}+4) \\\\ (3{a}_{2}+2)t+(3{b}_{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-3 &=0 \\\\ 3{a}_{2}+2 &=0 \\\\ 2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}+4 &={a}_{1} \\\\ 3{b}_{2}-1 &={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nthe first two equations, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{11}{6}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{9},\\,{b}_{1}=-\\frac{41}{36}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{41}{36} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308359 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1/2 & 3/2 \\\\ 3/2 & -1/2\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\right)+\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+\\frac{3}{2}({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})-\\frac{1}{2}({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-\\frac{{a}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{a}_{2}}{2}+1)t+(-\\frac{{b}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{b}_{2}}{2}-3) \\\\ (\\frac{3{a}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}}{2}+2)t+(\\frac{3{b}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}}{2}+1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{a}_{2}}{2}+1 &=0 \\\\ \\frac{3{a}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}}{2}+2 &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{b}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{b}_{2}}{2}-3 &={a}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{3{b}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}}{2}+1 &={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first two equations, we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-\\frac{7}{4},\\,{a}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-\\frac{11}{8},\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{11}{8} \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186308435 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}\\right) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ y &={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.24$ | $0$ | $0.11$ |\n| $y$ | $4.48$ | $1$ | $0.22$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/39/3981c3c5bdfc66850798ee3c34031acb0d1cdfea7181eb34a0a0422b44cc2ef0.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f2b5b95167b1ecd07aa1152aeac6d