matrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d0/d03f88d2eab48a0557f05311cd0d13d956b2af954277d322fabc4091f3aef2d4.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=-t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/e0/e0748b246241a44132c526d1419d301ec67579c1d1cfbac0854c8bfccea67dff.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/66a32254dcc579eb248d8691d0c079815e29b59c650fc4b38eaa47bfb1525ddc.png)"] [17592186308661 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a70188cf5de7e7b645