ince ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bff5cf9d21f0b01571b577ebe3d98ef08525d13a70a1d7379b537f6ba5319613.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4t+3 \\\\ y &=2t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/7d/7d590ee38f7f1b6b43c9b650890f42fa31b4c15951dab8bcb003b1ac1cc45c39.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-4t-3 \\\\ y &=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/b9/b965e3e244bc49b5e0f52840550c2ae997a0e38dc40e8757f0f51905c448d7af.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png)"] [17592186308721 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\left(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right)+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"] [17592186308722 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\left(u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"] [17592186311546 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\left(u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right)+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186311547 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\left(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right)+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186311548 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\left(u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186314969 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{a}-5\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ -5\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{5}(\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3-1=-4.$"] [17592186317510 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186318982 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(F),$ we reduce the matrix $F$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 19\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$rd columns from the original matrix are ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186319046 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ -2\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b}-6\\mathbf{a} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{b}-6\\mathbf{a}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -48 \\\\ -58\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 24 \\\\ 29\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=24-29=-5.$"] [17592186319174 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}\\left(\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\\right)=2.\n$$"] [17592186321062 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}\\left(\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\\right)=1.\n$$"] [17592186321065 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}\\left(\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\\right)=2.\n$$"] [17592186323745 "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Z) & ={z}_{11}{C}_{11}+{z}_{12}{C}_{12}+{z}_{13}{C}_{13}+{z}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & ={C}_{11}-{C}_{13} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ ∗ & 4 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & -1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & ∗ & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\right)-\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+0-(0-0+0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2((-3)\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-31) \\\\  & =-62\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323746 "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & ={u}_{11}{C}_{11}+{u}_{12}{C}_{12}+{u}_{13}{C}_{13}+{u}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {C}_{11}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =5{C}_{11}-2{C}_{12} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 2 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & -2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗ & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & ∗ & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & ∗ & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+2\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =5(0-(6\\cdot (-4)-1\\cdot 2)+0)+2(0-(3\\cdot (-4)-1\\cdot (-3))+0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 26+2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =130+18 \\\\  & =148\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323920 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(G-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that its augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"] [17592186323925 "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{69} & =P{D}^{69}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{69}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{69} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{69}\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323927 "We have been given the following diagonalization of $N.$\n\n$$\nN=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[9]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}\\underbrace{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $N=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${N}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{N}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{18} & =P{D}^{18}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[9]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{18}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[9]{2})^{18}\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 20 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-14 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 19\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323931 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}42 \\\\ -84\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{2}.$"] [17592186323943 "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSetting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-2z.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x-3(-2z)+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x+9z & =0 \\\\ x & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is $\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\right\\}.$"] [17592186323944 "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSetting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{12}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=-z.$ \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}z,\\,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$"] [17592186323947 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{3}.$"] [17592186323954 "Since $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $\\,T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}=4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =T\\left(4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=4T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323972 "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a horizontal shear of shear factor $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](images/4b/4b6cfc485ffa74f141e3dc4b8fad61b48103808a4f4c29b2dc5180f634902c4f.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326730 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}8 & -4 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & 3 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & \\frac{7}{6} & \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{6} & \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{5}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186326786 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\right)}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 22\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}22 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326787 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the 